/**
 * riya-game-platformer — riya the dot escapes the rail. In riya-game she was a bead slid ALONG the graphs; here the graphs are the landscape and she runs and jumps across them on foot. Twenty-eight worlds, one per math idea, all composed from the std game substrate — platformer-body (run/jump physics, one-way AABB terrain collision, pit-fall signal, curve-skating) + objective-flow (win gate) + fx-particles — rendered through math-canvas so the axes, the numbers, and the world's own function curve stay visible at all times. The arc: 1) The Plain — the number line as flat ground with pits; x is a number you stand on. 2) The Ramp — a staircase rising 1 per 2 across; slope is how fast you climb. 3) The Valley — y = x²/16 sampled into bars; you drop into the parabola and jump back up its walls. 4) The Mountain — a climb to the one summit star: the local maximum, where the slope flattens to zero. 5) The Pole — the ground rises toward a chasm at x = 3 that swallows you: the asymptote, the x the world never touches. 6) The Doubling Stair — steps of 1, 2, 4, 8: each costs double, and the last wall is too tall for your legs unless you find the springboard. 7) The Gallery — a Riemann skyline: the bars ARE the function, and you walk across its area. 8) Shifts — the same valley moved right 4: (x−4) transforms the function. 9) Corner — y = |x|/2: the slope jumps from −½ to +½ at a point with no slope. 10) Inflection — x³: steep, flat, steep; the slope's slope flips sign. 11) Log — ln(x+7): a burst that flattens forever, forbidden left of x = −7. 12) Derivative — the ramp you skate IS world 3's slope, drawn beside its ghost. 13) Area — the shaded region piles up behind you as you run: integration. 14) Accumulation — a hill whose height collects a dashed bump. 15) Decay — exponential decay toward a floor you never touch (y′ = −ky). 16) Tail — 1 + 12/(x+12): an infinite tail whose area stays finite (improper integral). 17) Series — steps 1, ½, ¼, … that never cross the ceiling 2 (geometric series). 18) Taylor — a line and a cubic hugging the sine wave you skate. 19) Vectors — every tread is the same displacement ⟨1.6, 1⟩; walking adds vectors tip-to-tail. 20) Contours — the skyline is a slice of the hill z = 7 − x²/16, with level lines z = 2, 4, 6 behind it. 21) Partials — the plane z = 3 + x/4 + y/2 slopes ¼ under your feet and ½ in the dashed direction. 22) Gradient — purple ∇f arrows always aim at the summit of the gaussian hill. 23) Saddle — the pass is the trail's minimum and the cross-trail's maximum at once. 24) Volume — each column is one sum; the climb across is the second sum: ∬ f dA. Every world: catch every ★ to clear; fall in a pit and riya climbs back at her spawn with every star kept — no punishment, this is a beginner's mountain Each world is its own atom under riya/atoms (riya-level-*) owning its entity contract, knobs, and page; this organism only rebinds them.
 *
 * Autogenerated by almadar-sync std-ts from
 * packages/almadar-std/behaviors/registry/organisms/riya-game-platformer.orb.
 *
 * This is a thin descriptor layer over the canonical .orb. The exported
 * functions return TraitReference / PageRefObject / OrbitalDefinition
 * payloads that reference the sibling .orb via `uses:` + `ref:`. The
 * compiler's inline phase materialises the real trait at build time.
 *
 * Do not hand-edit — regenerate via
 *   npx almadar-sync std-ts --only=riya-game-platformer
 *
 * @level organism
 * @packageDocumentation
 */

